# CAR, CRVAL = (10, 0), LONPOLE = 90, LATPOLE = 30 (synthetic). celset
# puts the native north pole at celestial (280, 30) and the south pole at
# (100, -30). Each native pole is a whole row of pixels in CAR, so the
# sky->pixel answer for a sky point exactly at one is a convention: wcslib
# sphs2x takes the "change of origin of longitude" (phi = phip + dlng - 180
# when 90 - latp < 90). Three SKY points sit exactly on the native poles;
# three more lie 1e-7 deg from them, where the native longitude rests on
# sphs2x's rearranged formula for |x| < 1e-5 (without it, 8e-5 px off).
# The grid crosses the rest of the sphere.
NOTE pipeline: sky points exactly at the native poles (wcslib sphs2x convention)
GRID 1 512 9 1 512 9
SKY 280 30
SKY 100 -30
SKY -80 30
SKY 280 30.0000001
SKY 280.0000001 30
SKY 100.0000001 -30.0000001
SKYGRID 0 350 8 -80 80 5
HEADER
NAXIS   = 2
NAXIS1  = 512
NAXIS2  = 512
CTYPE1  = 'RA---CAR'
CTYPE2  = 'DEC--CAR'
CRVAL1  = 10.0
CRVAL2  = 0.0
CRPIX1  = 256.5
CRPIX2  = 256.5
CDELT1  = -0.1
CDELT2  = 0.1
LONPOLE = 90.0
LATPOLE = 30.0
